*   **Work Supplies ($720):** Because you have freelance/1099 income, you can deduct this fully as a business expense against your $11,200 data analysis income. *(Note: W-2 employees can no longer deduct unreimbursed work expenses, so this only applies to your freelance work).*
*   **Charitable Donation ($1,100):** Because you will be taking the standard deduction, you unfortunately cannot deduct this charitable contribution. 

### 3. How to Handle the Home Office
Since you have self-employment income, you **can** claim the home office deduction against your freelance business (W-2 employees cannot claim a home office). You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot. (155 sq ft × $5 = **$775 deduction**)
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (155 sq ft / 680 sq ft = **~22.79%**). You can deduct 22.79% of your rent ($1,820 × 12 months = $21,840/year). 22.79% of $21,840 is **$4,977**. You can also add 22.79% of your utilities, renter's insurance, and internet. 
*   *Verdict:* The **Actual Expenses Method** will give you a significantly larger deduction, though it requires a bit more record-keeping.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, you will need the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** Your main tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule B:** Because your combined interest ($2,340) and dividends ($440) exceed $1,500, you must report them here.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $11,200 freelance income and deduct your $720 in work supplies.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** To calculate and claim your home office deduction using the actual expenses method.
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net freelance profit.

*Disclaimer: I am an AI assistant, not a CPA. It is always highly recommended to use tax software or consult a tax professional to ensure everything is filed accurately based on your exact Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI).*
